 (WIT) Recognition MIT Excellence Awards How to Nominate Nomination Examples Nomination Questions Tips for Writing a Nomination Award Categories Award Recipients FAQs Award Ceremony DLCI Programs Ways to Say Thank You Direct Appreciation Direct Appreciation Graphics Kudoboard Thank an MIT Community Member Thank You to These Community Members The Gordon Y. Billard Award The MIT Staff Award for Distinction in Service Disability Services & Leaves Accessibility for Staff Events Request Accommodations for an Event Sick Time Tracking Absence Type Codes for Support Staff Temporary Parking Temporary Parking Accommodation Request Obtaining Accommodations Forms & Resources Complaint Process and Resolution Initial Steps Resources Informal Options for Addressing a Concern The I-9 Process at MIT On Campus Verification Remote Verification Choose Your I-9 Authorized Representative Reverification E-verify For DLC Administrators Required Notices Labor Condition Application Notices Work Succeeding FAQs Guidelines on Providing Equipment for Working Remotely Mentoring for MIT Staff Resources on Mentoring Personal Information Center Address Change for Non Employees Name Change Content for Managers Hiring at MIT Advertising Request Applicant Tracking System Onboarding & Transitions Checklists for Managers Onboarding in Remote/Hybrid Work Environments Temporary Help Find a Temporary Employee Working with MITemps on Short-Term Hires Using VNDLY Types of Non-Academic Appointments The New Hire's Experience Recruiting and Hiring Guidance Managing & Developing Staff How Jobs and Pay are Structured at MIT Annual Salary Review (ASR) Job and Position Descriptions Merit Increases MIT Pay Guidelines MIT Pay Structure Pay Equity Mentoring for MIT Staff Performance Development Talent Planning and More Employee Recognition Talent Planning Organizational Development Consulting Supporting Employee Career Development Developing Your Team Developing Yourself as a Manager Resources for MIT Managers Developing Your Staff and Teams Engaging Staff and Teams Fostering Culture Change New Employees Getting Started Before You Start Your First Days and Weeks Your First Months Finding Your Way Policies Senior Leader Onboarding MIT's Structure and Governance MIT's Culture and Community Suggested Reading Leadership and Organization Development Retirees Health and Medical Plans 65 and Over Under 65 Medicare Prescription Drug Plan Continuing Care at MIT Health Dental Plan Flexible Spending Account Life Insurance HR Partners HR Partners Resource Guide Crisis Support External Career Counselors External Coaches, Consultants, and Trainers Layoffs Progressive Discipline Terminations and Offboarding HR HQ Events News Get Help In the Office Conference Rooms Safety and Security Recognition HR Infinite Mile Award Spot Appreciation Award Nomination Form Templates and Tools Reports Tech Help and Resources Events What's new Atlas HR team Contact Search Jobs Site Search Search Home Your Career Your Career Career Planning & Development Career Planning and Development MIT provides comprehensive career development services to help you think about your career and development plan at the Institute, including workshops and virtual individual consulting. Upcoming Career-Related Events Networking/Storytelling for Your Career Thursday, September 24, 2026 1:00 pm to 3:00 pm NE49-5145B NE49-5145 L-Lab Register Career Development: Assess Your Skills Thursday, October 8, 2026 10:00 am to 11:30 am Online Register Getting Unstuck in Your Career Thursday, November 19, 2026 1:00 pm to 2:30 pm Online Register See all career transition events Exploring Career Opportunities at MIT Use MIT's internal job search portal to see open positions. This helps department hiring managers easily identify you as an internal candidate with highly valued MIT experience. (NOTE: At this time, MIT has paused hiring for all non-essential positions.) Explore program management and project management career paths: If you're interested in a career in program or project management, this new online course is designed to help you plan for your professional development and career growth at MIT. The guide introduces you to job families and job roles at the Institute that focus on program management and project management, and describes the job duties needed to be successful. As you explore the different job families at MIT, you will be able to reflect on your experiences and current skills, and identify skills that you would like to develop. Explore admin assistant career paths: Administrative assistants, this online course is designed to help you plan for your professional development and career growth at MIT. The guide introduces you to various job families and job roles at the Institute and describes the job duties needed to be successful. As you explore the different job families at MIT, you will be able to reflect on your experiences and current skills, and identify skills that you would like to develop. Career Services for MIT Employees Individual consultations Individual consultations are available to employees on a limited basis by appointment. The consultations provide basic advice for individuals focused on their career at MIT, are typically 1-3 meetings, and could include the following topics as needed: Assess skills, interests, and values. Explore new roles and career paths through informational interviewing and job search preparation. Provide guidance on creating a development plan for your career goals. Prepare for a development discussion with your manager. Discuss options for continuing education and professional development. Develop strategies to manage common career stage transitions at early career, mid-career, or late career and retirement. We can consult with you virtually or by phone. Let us know what works for you. Email careerdevelopment@mit.edu to learn more or to schedule an appointment. Staff Career Connections Slack workspace Join our Slack workspace Staff Career Connections (formerly the Informational Interview Network on Yammer). Members use this space to share information about job fields, positions, departments, and their career experiences through informational interviews with other members. The workspace is also where you can find and share: Resources to help with informational interviewing; Ideas, new trends, information, and best practices related to career development; Articles, podcasts, and other resources; and Updates on MIT workshops and events related to career development. Find a guide to using the new workspace and other informational interviewing tools below . Podcast Series on Career Journeys at the Institute A career can have many different paths – and each one is unique. Listen as Michele King Harrington, Career Development Program Administrator in Human Resources, talks with MIT employees about their career journeys at the Institute. Discover their helpful advice for your own career development planning. Find the podcasts here. More Career Development Support How can I get help updating my résumé? We offer a workshop on creating and improving résumés, cover letters, and CVs. Keep an eye on the workshops on our Learn & Grow page for upcoming sessions. You may also wish to explore the resources from MIT's Career Advising and Professional Development Office. Where can I find professional development opportunities? Share your aspirations during your 1:1 with your manger. They may be able to share opportunities available within your team or department. See our Learn and Grow page for upcoming workshops on a variety of professional development topics Browse the resources available in the Atlas Learning Center . In addition to our own course catalog, you can explore LinkedIn Learning and edX.org . Take advantage of MIT's generous tuition reimbursement program to help you build or improve the skills you need to develop your career at MIT. How can I assess my skills in preparation for applying for a new role at MIT? Ask for feedback from your manager and colleagues, and review prior performance reviews to spot areas for improvement. Attend a \"Career Development: Assess Your Skills\" workshop. Watch the Learn & Grow page for upcoming sessions. MIT provides comprehensive career development consulting to help you think about your career and development plan at MIT. We can consult with you virtually or by phone – let us know what works for you. Email careerdevelopment@mit.edu to learn more or schedule an appointment. You may also access a consultation with a career coach to help you or a household member with career exploration and job search questions by contacting MyLifeServices . Related Forms & Resources Column headers with buttons are sortable. All column headers are sortable. Name Resource Type File Type Guide to the Staff Career Connections Slack Workspace Resources PDF Informational Interviews: An Overview Resources PDF Informational Interviews: Sample Questions Resources PDF Informational Interviews: What to Expect as an Interviewee Resources PDF All forms & resources Senior Caregiving Newsletter Work-Life offers a monthly newsletter, Senior Caregiving: Resources and Support for You and Your Family , specifically for individuals who are taking care of adults or elder loved ones. It features an article on caregiving from Bright Horizons, as well as MIT resources that can help support you and your family. Subscribe MIT HR Logo, return to homepage Human Resources Massachusetts Institute of Technology Building NE49-5000 600 Technology Square Cambridge, MA 02139 Contact Events Linkedin HR Team Accessibility Working together to build a better world. Search Term Search Search Jobs Forms & Resources Health Plans Institute Holidays Life Events MyLife Services"
    },
