our Staff and Teams Engaging Staff and Teams Fostering Culture Change New Employees Getting Started Before You Start Your First Days and Weeks Your First Months Finding Your Way Policies Senior Leader Onboarding MIT's Structure and Governance MIT's Culture and Community Suggested Reading Leadership and Organization Development Retirees Health and Medical Plans 65 and Over Under 65 Medicare Prescription Drug Plan Continuing Care at MIT Health Dental Plan Flexible Spending Account Life Insurance HR Partners HR Partners Resource Guide Crisis Support External Career Counselors External Coaches, Consultants, and Trainers Layoffs Progressive Discipline Terminations and Offboarding HR HQ Events News Get Help In the Office Conference Rooms Safety and Security Recognition HR Infinite Mile Award Spot Appreciation Award Nomination Form Templates and Tools Reports Tech Help and Resources Events What's new Atlas HR team Contact Search Jobs Site Search Search Home Your Career Learn & Grow Learn & Grow Managers' Feedback Program Managers' Feedback Program MIT managers are invited to join this program on asking for, receiving, and using feedback to improve effectiveness and augment growth. Overview Feedback is a gift whose value is best realized when we embrace it with an open mind, commit to action based on it, and follow through in the spirit of continual self-improvement. Open to all MIT managers of people , the program consists of four sessions and independent, individual work over the course of four months. After an introductory session on how to receive feedback, participants will ask for feedback from two direct reports and based on that feedback, set and pursue management learning goals. Pair check-ins over the course of the program provide opportunities for the participants to track their progress and support each other as a cohort. Who May Participate? Any MIT manager of people More About the Program Program benefits The program is intended to provide managers with the experience of asking for feedback from their direct reports and setting and working toward development goals based on that feedback. It is based in a recognition that: Managers serve a critical role in guiding work and creating a culture of respect and inclusion. There can be a gap between what we intend with our words and actions and the impact we are having on others. Feedback can help managers be mindful of that possible gap. Others see things about our performance that we don't see. Feedback gives managers the benefit of their direct reports' perspectives. We are all a work in progress. Feedback can help managers with their ongoing learning and development. The practice of asking for and receiving feedback with an open mind and a learning orientation can enhance communication and trust between a manager and direct report. What to expect Managers who participate in the program commit to the following expectations: Attend all four sessions Complete self-directed activities Ask for and be open to honest feedback from their direct reports Embrace feedback as a learning opportunity Commit to continual learning as a manager Support fellow participants as they seek to learn from feedback and grow as managers Provide feedback to the program facilitator on the program experience and opportunities for improvement Program sequence Month 1 Workshop: Feedback as a Gift (2 hours) Program overview Receiving feedback Learning from feedback Months 1-2 Self-Directed: Gathering Feedback Managers meet with two direct reports to ask for and hear feedback (questions, guidance provided) Managers aggregate and reflect on feedback Managers prepare to bring feedback to the next workshop Month 2 Workshop: Feedback as a First Step (2 hours) Debrief feedback from direct reports Development goal-setting and action planning Months 2-3 Self-Directed: Sharing Goals Managers meet with their two direct reports to share the development goals they have set Month 3 Progress Update and Peer Coaching Session (2 hours) Month 3 Self-Directed: Direct Report Status Updates and Iteration Managers meet with their two direct reports to ask for feedback on their progress Managers iterate on goals based on additional feedback Month 4 Workshop: Feedback as a Strategy (2 hours) Debrief work on development goals and program experience Debrief all feedback from direct reports Plan for how to use feedback as a management strategy moving forward Session schedule Fall 2026 sessions will take place in NE49-5000 (MIT Human Resources) and on Zoom. Session Date Time Location Session 1 Tuesday, September 8 10 a.m. to 12 p.m. NE49-5000, L-Lab Session 2 Tuesday, October 20 10 a.m. to 12 p.m. Zoom Session 3 Tuesday, November 17 10 a.m. to 12 p.m. Zoom Session 4 Tuesday, December 15 10 a.m. to 12 p.m. NE49-5000, L-Lab Learn More and Register Learn more and register here. Questions? Email learn@mit.edu . MIT HR Logo, return to homepage Human Resources Massachusetts Institute of Technology Building NE49-5000 600 Technology Square Cambridge, MA 02139 Contact Events Linkedin HR Team Accessibility Working together to build a better world. Search Term Search Search Jobs Forms & Resources Health Plans Institute Holidays Life Events MyLife Services"
    },
