g Your Staff and Teams Engaging Staff and Teams Fostering Culture Change New Employees Getting Started Before You Start Your First Days and Weeks Your First Months Finding Your Way Policies Senior Leader Onboarding MIT's Structure and Governance MIT's Culture and Community Suggested Reading Leadership and Organization Development Retirees Health and Medical Plans 65 and Over Under 65 Medicare Prescription Drug Plan Continuing Care at MIT Health Dental Plan Flexible Spending Account Life Insurance HR Partners HR Partners Resource Guide Crisis Support External Career Counselors External Coaches, Consultants, and Trainers Layoffs Progressive Discipline Terminations and Offboarding HR HQ Events News Get Help In the Office Conference Rooms Safety and Security Recognition HR Infinite Mile Award Spot Appreciation Award Nomination Form Templates and Tools Reports Tech Help and Resources Events What's new Atlas HR team Contact Search Jobs Site Search Search Home Your Career Performance Development Performance Development Development & Career Planning Development & Career Planning MIT values learning and development both professionally and personally. Development and career planning is a rich opportunity for managers and employees to plan for and support an individual's growth and development with an eye toward the department and Institute’s current and future needs. In addition to the best practices and tools below, see our career development resources for employees and managers . Best Practices Identifies opportunities to contribute to broader organizational priorities Identifies the knowledge, skills, and experience to expand an employee’s contributions and career Incorporates employee interests, preferences, and strengths Creates an Individual Development Plan ( below ) that identifies and tracks a wide range of development activities ( below ) Where applicable, incorporates an Individual Career Plan ( below ) Makes use of MIT’s opportunities to learn and grow Learning and Growth Opportunities There are many different ways to learn and grow at MIT. Below are some ideas to incorporate into development planning and learning. Action-oriented activities Work on department or Institute teams, projects or committee Identify opportunities to learn through job shadowing, cross-training, teamwork, and new assignments Set goals and keep a log for practicing a new behavior or skill Lead team/department discussions about current work projects Give presentations about work projects to colleagues and/or at conferences Propose and organize learning opportunities for others Provide training to colleagues on a topic or skill Organize an Independent Activities Period (IAP) offering Join and be active in trade associations and professional networks related to your field People-oriented activities Ask someone – manager, peer, colleague, for feedback on progress towards goals Partner with someone to build skills, share lessons learned, and give and receive feedback Find a mentor, coach, or someone in a job or career of interest to interview Volunteer to be a student advisor or for MIT’s Mentor Advocate Partnership (MAP) Find a subject matter expert and interview them. Ask how they developed their skills. Observe how others effectively demonstrate the behavior or skills you are looking to develop Connect with groups at MIT that focus on networking and skill development; for example, MIT’s Toastmasters Clubs, Employee Resource Groups (ERGs), and the MIT Public Service Center Volunteer and attend MIT-wide events, including MIT’s Excellence Awards , MIT Commencement, and the MIT Martin Luther King Jr. breakfast Create an “affinity group” by bringing together a group of MIT colleagues with similar interests Create a book group by inviting colleagues to read and discuss a relevant book or article Knowledge-oriented activities Attend an MIT workshop or program Explore free, online learning through MIT Learning Bundles, edX, GlobeSmart, LinkedIn Learning, and Skillsoft Attend a Seminar Series through MIT HR Work-Life Take advantage of free, one-on-one career coaching through MIT’s Career Development Services Explore career consultation through MIT MyLife Services Apply MIT’s Tuition Assistance Plan to courses related to your job or career or to career counseling Read a book or article. Find a book or article through the MIT Libraries or Skillsoft Take a course through MIT’s Professional Education Program or MIT Sloan’s Executive Education Program Enroll in MIT’s STEM classes as a non-degree student through the MIT Advanced Study Program (ASP) Advance your leadership through The Partnership, Inc ., or Conexion Online Learning Career Conversations (Workshop) Career Conversations for Managers (Workshop) Developing Oneself (Learning Bundle) Developing the Potential of Others (Learning Bundle) Exploring Mentoring for Mentor and Mentee (Learning Bundle) Networking Skills (Learning Bundle) Tools and Resources Current Performance Reflection Worksheet Development Planning Worksheet Individual Career Plan Individual Development Plan Job Requirements Worksheet Learning Recap Worksheet See additional tools for managers MIT HR Logo, return to homepage Human Resources Massachusetts Institute of Technology Building NE49-5000 600 Technology Square Cambridge, MA 02139 Contact Events Linkedin HR Team Accessibility Working together to build a better world. Search Term Search Search Jobs Forms & Resources Health Plans Institute Holidays Life Events MyLife Services"
    },
